BRU/OS-9 (1M)                 OS-9 Utility                 BRU/OS-9 (1M)^bNAME^b      BRU/OS-9 - A UNIX (tm) like Backup/Restore Utility ^bUSAGE^b      Backup:           ^bbru -c [options] pathname(s)^b      Restore:           ^bbru -x [options] [pattern(s)]^b      File Listing:           ^bbru -t [options] [pattern(s)]^b ^bOPTIONS^b      ^b-f^b  =  use  the specified file as the backup medium; the default is     '/D1/BRUfile'      ^b-v^b = be verbose (more v's gives more information)      ^b-q^b = be quiet (don't prompt for new disks)      ^b-n^b = only handle files newer than the given date (YY/MM/DD) or file      ^b-s^b = /dd/dirname/filename, usable with -c option, starts partial          backup at 'filename'^bDESCRIPTION^b      One of the most important (and most often neglected) duties of  the     hard drive owner is the backup process.  Until now, there have been      relatively  few  options  to choose from to accomplish this dreaded     task.  BRU/OS-9 is a simple, easy to use backup and restore utility      for your hard drive.  It uses  all  the  available  space  on  your     backup  device  (usually  a  floppy  drive, but it can be any drive     and/or file) and is self-contained so  that  you  do  not  need  to     remember  some  cryptic  sequence  of  commands to 'pipe' together.     BRU/OS-9 was modeled  after  the  UNIX(tm)  backup/restore  utility     ^bbru^b.  BRU/OS-9 is not compatible in any way with it, however.  ^bBUGS AND CAVEATS^b .cp4     When  recreating  directories,  only  the  directory attributes and     ownership are restored.  The file creation and  modification  times     reflect  the  current  date and time; this is due to limitations of     directory file access permitted by RBF.  .cp4     When doing a backup or  restoration  using  the  '-n'  option,  all     directories  affected  are  backed-up or restored regardless of the     modification time.  This is done to ensure  proper  restoration  of     directory ownership and attributes.  .cp4     BRU/OS-9 presently  cannot  restore  file  links.  If a file with a     link count greater than one (1) is  found,  a  warning  message  is     displayed and  the  file  is  archived.    On restoration, multiple     copies of the file will be recreated.  .cp6     BRU/OS-9 can handle a maximum of 11 directory levels.  This is  due     to  the 16 file per process limit imposed by OS-9/6809. No checking     is performed to detect this overflow (system errors will  halt  the     backup process).       See the file 'COPYING' for warranty and licensing information.  .cp6^bAUTHORS^b 1.0  Michael R. Sweet         1.1, 1.2  Gene Heskett     23W Kennedy Plaza                  291 Garton Avenue      Utica, NY 13502                    Weston, WV 26330     Delphi: DODGECOLT                  WOAY                                 .bpUPDATED                    BRU version 1.1, 1.2                 GENE HESKETTChanges from 1.0:     Changed the path used in the get keypress of bru_disk() from stderr          to stdin. The use of stderr as an input path caused a fall-thru          loop on my machine. Instead of patiently waiting for a new disk          it ran a loop of looking at the disk, finding it was full, etc.     Added  some  code  for  crash recovery  or  restart  for  whatever     reason.. BRU now has a -s option.     To use:          bru -cvvvvs /dd/mail/myletter[ENTER]     Which will run bru as usual but writing to the output will be dis-     abled  until  that file is encountered. I put this in  for  system     crashs 'while it was running bru previously' recovery. Do a bru -t     to  see  how far it got before crashing, and start it on the  last     file  name displayed by the output from the -t option  run.     This  way,  a  new backup can be started from where  the  old  one     crashed.     Also, the backup medium pathname is now '/D1/BRUfile' by default.11/22/92 adds.     And now, as of 11/22/92, the target device/filename is now able to     be  reassigned  on  a semi permanent basis by makeing  a  file  in     /dd/sys  whose  contents are the desired "/device/filename\n"  for     bru  to  use.   Now those users who don't use /d1 as  the  default     device  won't have to keep using the -f option for everything they     do.   If  the  file doesn't exist, operation is as  before  useing     /d1/brufile  as  the  archive  media path. The  "\n"  indicates  a     carraige   return  for  those  not  familiar  with  "C"  notation.     Wherever  you see that in a text string, replace the "backslash n"     with the ENTER key.